  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 314 AM EDT Thu Aug 13 2026\n\nEarly this morning, GOES-East Nighttime Microphysics RGB imagery \nshows high cirrus emanating from mesoscale systems in the Plains. \nCAMs show chances of any precipitation straying over the UP this \nmorning at approximately 10 percent or less. With a not particularly \nmoist airmass over the UP (PWAT a little under an inch is around the \nclimatological median for mid August) and clouds aloft to decrease \nthe efficiency of radiational cooling, not expecting any fog that \nforms overnight to become anything more than isolated patches.\n\nBy 12Z this morning, the global deterministic 00Z suite still \nmaintains decent agreement on the 500mb pattern. Upper air pattern \nconsists of a Gulf Coast ridge flanked by troughing over both North \nAmerican oceanic coasts supported by deeper troughing or closed lows \nover Alberta and Quebec respectively. While good agreement should be \nexpected at model hour 12, it also should be expected at model hour \n30, which surprisingly there is a significant divergence between the \nglobal deterministics in the central Canada to Great Lakes region. \nFor the most part, the models agree that the western trough \nfragments into either 1 or 2 closed lows over western Canada and \nthen a trough over Manitoba to northern Ontario that swings near the \nnorthern periphery of the Great Lakes before eventually merging with \nthe East Coast broad trough. However, for being within 2 days of \nmodel initiation, the global deterministics are widely spread in how \nsharp the near-Great Lakes trough is and at what time and track the \ntrough passes. What this translates to is that if the trough is \nweaker, later, and/or passes north/east of the Great Lakes, the Gulf \nCoast ridging will prevail and this evening will remain dry. \nHowever, if the trough is sharper, passes sooner, and/or near the \nGreat Lakes, the ridging will be suppressed enough for some \nprecipitation to occur, especially along the Great Lakes lake \nbreezes. What was looking like a slam-dunk dry day a few days ago is \nnow looking like up to 40% chances of precipitation for the central \nUP per the LREF and HREF, but there's enough moving parts and \nfailure mechanisms that forecaster confidence is much lower, so PoPs \nwill look a bit more like 15-25% today.\n\nEnsembles come into better agreement for Friday into early Saturday \nas ridging more firmly is established over the Great Lakes, leading \nto a dry day for the UP. By 12Z Saturday, mid-level troughing will \nbe over Saskatchewan/Manitoba and pass into northern Ontario by 12Z \nSunday. Ahead of this trough will be a broad area of 40-60% chances \nof precipitation. LREF mean QPF 12ZSat-12ZSun is widespread 0.15-\n0.25 inches, except for south of US-2 in Menominee/Delta Co.'s where \nthe mean is closer to 0.33 inches. The LREF 90th percentile presents \na reasonable wettest case scenario of a half inch to 1 inch of QPF \nfor Menominee County and the communities along Lake Michigan with a \nthird to a half inch for the rest of the UP. As much of the UP is \nrunning a half inch to an inch below the August normal precipitation \npace, even the high end of QPF is unlikely to present much threat \nfor flooding. Thunderstorm chances are also a bit uncertain, as the \nLREF puts chances of 250+ J/kg of SBCAPE at around 15% along the \nstate line Saturday and below 10% elsewhere.\n\nWhile there is understandable spread on the pattern that follows, it \nis generally expected that by Sunday evening and through Monday \nnight, ridging resumes in the wake of the trough. Next Tuesday \nlooking to resume the ongoing pattern of weak precip signals in \npersistent NWerly flow, unless the ensembles can coalesce around the \nglobal deterministic models which each have their own idea of how \nthe closed low aloft over Hudson Bay may gyrate south enough into \nnorthern Ontario to bring decent enough PVA to support showers and \nstorms.",
